      "statusChangeID": null,
      "value": "The main goal of the current offline avatar system is that the avatar works exactly like it would if handed out via the live service. I think the request to ignore limits could easily be in the file name. \n\nWhere this request likely falls flat is the actual physbones implementation. I could see them wasting a bit of ram in the name of speed. If the amount of ram allocated towards physbones on an avatar is static, don't need to have the code think about memory allocation while loading. If it fits, it fits, if it somehow doesn't, stop.\n\nBut then it you try to come back and exceed that, now you gotta consider if enough ram has been allocated...",
      "viewerReactions": null,
      "voteID": null
    }
  ],
  "companyID": "58c62e995865713647a7115d",
  "created": "2023-09-30T10:28:03.489Z",
  "deletedAt": null,
  "deletedBy": null,
  "details": "I like to take pictures in VRC, and for that I have lots of different outfits. The problem is I can't upload my avatar anymore as there's a hard limit on phys bone components. Even if I use a version of sdk that doesn't restrict uploads, phys bones will be disabled in game anyway. This hugely limits creativity.\n\nSplitting avatar into multiple versions is simply inconvenient and managing a lot of versions is painful and time consuming. Sometimes you wanna change the outfit after you already made a pose / angle, setup vrc lens, but changing avatar will reset everything.\n\nThere's no reason to enforce these limits on offline uploads as it won't affect anyone else's performance.",
